1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:05,045
Quick question.

2
00:00:05,065 --> 00:00:06,257
What note comes next?

3
00:00:07,877 --> 00:00:09,610
Your brain just made a guess.

4
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:14,324
And that's exactly what AI does, all day
long, with words, images, and sounds.

5
00:00:14,844 --> 00:00:16,982
AI doesn't know things the way you do.

6
00:00:17,002 --> 00:00:18,175
It predicts patterns.

7
00:00:18,695 --> 00:00:21,943
So today, we're borrowing
five moves from musicians,

8
00:00:21,963 --> 00:00:24,897
to help you use AI like
a pro, not a passenger.

9
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:31,209
First, a quick sound check.

10
00:00:31,529 --> 00:00:33,964
This video is for students 13 and up.

11
00:00:33,984 --> 00:00:38,543
Only use AI tools your school, or
a parent or guardian, has approved,

12
00:00:38,563 --> 00:00:40,733
and follow each tool's age rules.

13
00:00:41,153 --> 00:00:42,892
Then, try the billboard test.

14
00:00:42,912 --> 00:00:46,410
If you wouldn't put it on a
billboard outside your school,

15
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:47,927
don't type it into an AI.

16
00:00:47,947 --> 00:00:51,445
No full names, addresses,
passwords, or photos of friends.

17
00:00:52,867 --> 00:00:53,361
Move one.

18
00:00:53,381 --> 00:00:54,503
Guess the next note.

19
00:00:54,923 --> 00:00:56,798
Here's a real TomoClub session,

20
00:00:56,818 --> 00:01:01,077
where students built their own songs
in Chrome Music Lab's Song Maker.

21
00:01:01,097 --> 00:01:03,461
Listen to what the facilitator notices.

22
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:10,780
[Student's song plays]

23
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:11,780
[Facilitator] Nice.

24
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:13,780
I like the gap in between where it plays

25
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:16,580
and gives something to anticipate as well.

26
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:17,080
Well done.

27
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:19,099
Something to anticipate.

28
00:01:19,119 --> 00:01:20,365
That's the key idea.

29
00:01:20,385 --> 00:01:22,643
AI writes one small piece at a time,

30
00:01:22,663 --> 00:01:25,808
always predicting what's
most likely to come next.

31
00:01:26,228 --> 00:01:27,174
Try it yourself.

32
00:01:27,194 --> 00:01:31,401
Build three bars in Song Maker, then
ask a friend to guess the fourth.

33
00:01:31,421 --> 00:01:34,481
Notice how often they pick
the safe, expected note.

34
00:01:34,901 --> 00:01:36,075
AI plays it safe too.

35
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:38,122
So when you want something original,

36
00:01:38,142 --> 00:01:41,250
that's your cue to choose
the surprising note yourself.

37
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:43,259
Move two.

38
00:01:43,279 --> 00:01:44,159
Play it twice.

39
00:01:44,579 --> 00:01:47,994
Musicians replay a tricky
part to see if it holds up.

40
00:01:48,014 --> 00:01:49,290
Do the same with AI.

41
00:01:49,310 --> 00:01:53,243
Open a brand-new chat, and ask
the exact same question again.

42
00:01:53,663 --> 00:01:56,221
If the answers match, that's a good sign.

43
00:01:56,241 --> 00:01:59,741
If a name, number, or date
changes, the AI was guessing.

44
00:01:59,761 --> 00:02:02,633
Treat that part as a
rumor until you check it.

45
00:02:04,067 --> 00:02:04,717
Move three.

46
00:02:04,737 --> 00:02:05,509
Flip the key.

47
00:02:05,929 --> 00:02:10,400
Switch a song from a major key to a
minor key, and the whole mood changes.

48
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:12,098
Questions work the same way.

49
00:02:12,518 --> 00:02:14,800
Ask: why is studying
in the morning better?

50
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,903
Then, in a new chat, ask: why
is studying at night better?

51
00:02:18,323 --> 00:02:23,301
If the AI happily agrees both times, it's
following your lead, not the evidence.

52
00:02:23,321 --> 00:02:27,986
So ask neutral questions instead, like:
what are the pros and cons of each?

53
00:02:29,433 --> 00:02:30,023
Move four.

54
00:02:30,043 --> 00:02:31,305
Find the sheet music.

55
00:02:31,725 --> 00:02:34,999
Before you repeat a fact
from an AI, find the receipt.

56
00:02:35,019 --> 00:02:39,025
Open a real source yourself, like
a textbook or a trusted website,

57
00:02:39,045 --> 00:02:41,527
and find the exact line that backs it up.

58
00:02:41,947 --> 00:02:46,269
A link from an AI doesn't count until
you've clicked it and seen that line with

59
00:02:46,289 --> 00:02:47,039
your own eyes.

60
00:02:47,059 --> 00:02:47,643
No receipt?

61
00:02:47,663 --> 00:02:48,523
Don't repeat it.

62
00:02:49,967 --> 00:02:50,586
Move five.

63
00:02:50,606 --> 00:02:51,546
Play it by ear.

64
00:02:51,966 --> 00:02:56,146
A musician who can only play while reading
the sheet hasn't really learned the song

65
00:02:56,166 --> 00:02:56,348
yet.

66
00:02:56,768 --> 00:03:00,043
So after AI helps you with
something, close the tab,

67
00:03:00,063 --> 00:03:04,352
and explain it out loud in 30
seconds, to a friend, a family member,

68
00:03:04,372 --> 00:03:05,430
or even your dog.

69
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:09,332
Wherever you get stuck, that's
the part you still need to learn.

70
00:03:09,352 --> 00:03:11,685
AI should train your
brain, not replace it.

71
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:15,820
The students in this session
didn't aim for perfect.

72
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:16,792
They experimented.

73
00:03:17,783 --> 00:03:19,063
[Facilitator] Wow, wow. I love this.

74
00:03:19,083 --> 00:03:23,063
Yeah, that's how you experiment.

75
00:03:23,083 --> 00:03:25,563
That's how you experiment. Yes, I love it.

76
00:03:25,583 --> 00:03:27,063
Let's see how it would sound.

77
00:03:27,083 --> 00:03:28,463
[Student's song plays]

78
00:03:28,683 --> 00:03:31,163
I love the pattern in
which you have colored,

79
00:03:31,183 --> 00:03:33,163
shows that you have gone for some pattern.

80
00:03:33,183 --> 00:03:36,963
This is like pressing all the keys on
the keyboard together, so that's nice.

81
00:03:36,983 --> 00:03:39,063
Yeah, lovely.

82
00:03:39,183 --> 00:03:40,563
Like the experimentation.

83
00:03:40,883 --> 00:03:41,763
Wonderful team.

84
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:43,281
That's the mindset.

85
00:03:43,301 --> 00:03:45,832
Treat AI like an
instrument you're learning.

86
00:03:45,852 --> 00:03:48,730
Test it, question it, and
listen for its patterns.

87
00:03:50,267 --> 00:03:51,342
Now it's your turn.

88
00:03:51,362 --> 00:03:54,224
Here's today's challenge:
can you make an AI flip?

89
00:03:54,644 --> 00:03:58,607
Pick a this or that question, like
cats or dogs, summer or winter,

90
00:03:58,627 --> 00:04:00,176
or morning or night study.

91
00:04:00,596 --> 00:04:04,771
In one chat with an approved AI tool,
ask why the first one is better.

92
00:04:04,791 --> 00:04:08,666
Then open a brand-new chat, and
ask why the second one is better.

93
00:04:09,086 --> 00:04:13,380
Did your AI flip to agree with you
both times, or did it hold its ground?

94
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,394
Comment flipped, or held,
plus the pair you tested.

95
00:04:16,814 --> 00:04:18,156
And keep it anonymous.

96
00:04:18,176 --> 00:04:21,560
No names, schools, or personal
details in the comments.

97
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:24,303
Quick recap.

98
00:04:24,573 --> 00:04:25,581
Guess the next note.

99
00:04:25,801 --> 00:04:26,646
Play it twice.

100
00:04:26,866 --> 00:04:27,660
Flip the key.

101
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:28,973
Find the sheet music.

102
00:04:29,193 --> 00:04:30,233
And play it by ear.

103
00:04:31,967 --> 00:04:33,302
Oh, and one more thing.

104
00:04:33,322 --> 00:04:36,248
The voice you've been
hearing was generated by AI.

105
00:04:36,268 --> 00:04:38,133
Now you know how to question it.

106
00:04:38,553 --> 00:04:40,891
Stay curious, and we'll
see you in the comments.

